update public.moo_versions set body_md = body_md || E'| Price monitoring                        | DTI / City Market Office                     | Market monitoring personnel        | C / EOC information integration                 |\n| Business continuity outreach            | CDRRMO Head                                  | CDRRMO and Chamber of Commerce     | A / R for DRRM outreach                         |\n\nThis matrix ensures that the CDRRMO coordinates the overall resilience system without appropriating the mandates of other City offices.\n\n## 8.5 RACI Compliance Rules\n\nThe following rules shall govern all RACI matrices in the MOO:\n\n1.  Each activity shall have one clearly designated Accountable role.\n\n2.  Multiple Responsible roles may be assigned where work is genuinely shared.\n\n3.  Consulted roles shall be limited to those whose technical or institutional input is necessary.\n\n4.  Informed roles shall receive the relevant outputs without becoming unnecessary approval points.\n\n5.  RACI assignments shall be reviewed whenever the organizational structure, legal mandate or delegation changes.\n\n6.  Where a statutory body exercises collective authority, the Council or Sanggunian shall be identified as the accountable decision-making body, with the designated Chairperson responsible for convening and presiding over proceedings.\n\n7.  Each RACI matrix shall identify the approving authority for the final output.\n\nThe detailed job action sheets and position-level RACI matrices shall be developed under the organizational and EOC modules.\n\n# 9. Total Quality Management and Institutional Performance\n\n## 9.1 Quality Management Approach\n\nThe CDRRMO shall apply Total Quality Management principles to the design, implementation, monitoring and improvement of its governance processes.\n\nTQM shall be treated as an institutional management approach rather than a separate administrative activity. Its purpose is to ensure that DRRM services are reliable, timely, accountable, evidence-based and responsive to the needs of affected communities.\n\n![Figure 14: 9.1 Quality Management Approach](/library/ch1v12/fig-14.webp)\n\nThe following principles shall guide implementation:\n\n| **TQM principle**              | **Application to CDRRMO–EOC operations**                                                                                       |\n|--------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Customer and stakeholder focus | Decisions and services shall respond to the needs of communities, affected populations, responders and institutional partners. |\n| Leadership and accountability  | Each process shall have a designated owner and authorized decision-maker.                                                      |\n| Process orientation            | Activities shall be standardized through workflows, SOPs, checklists and defined outputs.                                      |\n| Systems approach               | Institutional processes shall be managed as interconnected functions rather than isolated activities.                          |\n| Evidence-based decisions       | Decisions shall be supported by verified risk information, assessment results, operational reports and documented evidence.    |\n| People engagement              | Personnel, barangays, CSOs and partners shall participate in process design, implementation and improvement.                   |\n| Continuous improvement         | Findings from exercises, incidents, audits and assessments shall result in corrective and preventive action.                   |\n| Relationship management        | Partnerships and institutional interfaces shall be actively maintained and evaluated.                                          |\n\n## 9.2 Plan–Do–Check–Act (PDCA) cycle\n\nThe City shall apply the PDCA cycle as the primary mechanism for institutional quality improvement.\n\n**Institutional Quality Management Cycle**\n\n![Figure 15: 9.2 Plan–Do–Check–Act (PDCA) cycle](/library/ch1v12/fig-15.webp)\n\nThe PDCA cycle shall operate continuously across all CDRRMO and EOC functional modules.\n\n## 9.3 Application of PDCA to institutional governance\n\n| **PDCA stage** | **Key activities**                                                                                    | **Responsible institutional actors**              | **Required evidence**                                     |\n|----------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|\n| Plan           | Annual governance calendar, policy review, risk-informed priorities, budget and staffing requirements | CDRRMO Head, CDRRMC, City Planning, Budget Office | Approved plans, meeting calendar, LDRRMFIP                |\n| Do             | Meetings, policy implementation, coordination, declaration procedures and institutional operations    | CDRRMO, Secretariat, City offices and partners    | Minutes, resolutions, SOP records, reports                |\n| Check          | Compliance assessment, performance monitoring, document verification and review of decisions          | CDRRMO, Internal Audit, CDRRMC                    | Dashboard, audit reports, compliance register             |\n| Act            | Corrective action, institutional restructuring, policy amendment and procedure revision               | Mayor, CDRRMC, SP and responsible offices         | Corrective action register, revised policies, updated MOO |\n\n## 9.4 Process Performance Indicators\n\nThe following indicators are proposed for Chapter 1. They shall be integrated into the broader MOO Monitoring and Evaluation Framework.\n\n| **Performance indicator**                                                          | **Proposed target**                        | **Frequency**        | **Process owner**  |\n|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| CDRRMC regular meetings conducted                                                  | At least 4 annually                        | Quarterly            | CDRRMC Secretariat |\n| CDRRMC membership register updated                                                 | 100% of prescribed seats documented        | Annual               | CDRRMO             |\n| CDRRMC resolutions with assigned owners and deadlines                              | 100%                                       | Every meeting        | Secretariat        |\n| CDRRMC action items with documented status                                         | 100% tracked                               | Quarterly            | CDRRMO             |\n| Critical DRRM policies subjected to legal review                                   | 100% before approval                       | Per policy           | City Legal Office  |\n| Critical governance procedures with approved SOPs                                  | 100% of identified mandatory procedures    | Annual review        | CDRRMO             |\n| State of Calamity recommendations with supporting RDANA                            | 100%                                       | Per occurrence       | CDRRMO             |\n| State of Imminent Disaster requests with complete PDRA documentation               | 100%                                       | Per applicable event | CDRRMO             |\n| State of Imminent Disaster post-lifting reports submitted within prescribed period | 100%                                       | Per declaration      | CDRRMO             |\n| CDRRMC decisions with documented follow-through                                    | 100% recorded                              | Quarterly            | CDRRMO Secretariat |\n| High-priority institutional corrective actions completed by approved deadlines     | At least 90%, with overdue items escalated | Quarterly            | CDRRMO Head        |\n\nThese are proposed management targets, not existing performance claims. They shall be calibrated during the validation workshop and approved through the City''s institutional performance arrangements.\n\n## 9.5 Corrective and Preventive Action\n\nThe CDRRMO shall maintain a Corrective and Preventive Action Register (CAPA) linked to:\n\n- Institutional Assessment Tool findings;\n\n- CDRRMC resolutions;\n\n- Internal and external audit findings;\n\n- After-action reviews;\n\n- Exercise evaluation reports;\n\n- Incident and response reports;\n\n- Stakeholder feedback; and\n\n- Applicable changes in laws and policies.\n\nEach corrective action shall identify:\n\n| **CAPA field**       | **Required information**                                                                   |\n|----------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Reference            | Source of the finding or non-conformity                                                    |\n| Problem statement    | Specific deficiency or process failure                                                     |\n| Root cause           | Underlying organizational, procedural, technical or resource-related cause                 |\n| Risk consequence     | Potential effect on life safety, service delivery, compliance or institutional performance |\n| Corrective action    | Specific intervention to address the deficiency                                            |\n| Accountable owner    | Single office or official responsible for completion                                       |\n| Responsible actors   | Personnel or offices undertaking the work                                                  |\n| Target date          | Approved completion deadline                                                               |\n| Required resources   | Budget, personnel, equipment or technical assistance                                       |\n| Verification         | Evidence that the action was implemented                                                   |\n| Effectiveness review | Evidence that the original problem has been resolved and has not recurred                  |\n\nA finding shall not be closed merely because an action has been initiated. Closure requires evidence of completion and verification of effectiveness.\n\n# 10. Alignment with the MCR2030 Ten Essentials\n\nThis chapter primarily contributes to the Governance and Financial Capacity cluster of the MCR2030 Ten Essentials while establishing the institutional foundation for the other two clusters.\n\n![Figure 16: 10. Alignment with the MCR2030 Ten Essentials](/library/ch1v12/fig-16.webp)\n\n| **MCR2030 Essential**                                              | **Chapter 1 contribution** | **Institutional mechanism**                                                             |\n|--------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| 1\\. Organize for Disaster Resilience                               | Primary                    | CDRRMC, CDRRMO, EOC mandates, authorities and coordination architecture.                |\n| 2\\. Identify, Understand and Use Current and Future Risk Scenarios | Supporting                 | Institutional responsibility for risk intelligence and evidence-based policy decisions. |\n| 3\\. Strengthen Financial Capability for Resilience                 | Supporting                 | Governance of DRRM investment, funding, accountability and resource mobilization.       |\n| 4\\. Pursue Resilient Urban Development and Design                  | Supporting                 | Institutional interface with CPDO, Zoning Administrator and City Engineer.              |\n| 5\\. Safeguard Natural Buffers to Enhance Protective Functions      | Supporting                 | Institutional interface with CENRO, DENR and watershed partners.                        |\n| 6\\. Strengthen Institutional Capacity for Resilience               | Primary                    | Institutional roles, accountability, staffing, competence and continuity.               |\n| 7\\. Understand and Strengthen Societal Capacity for Resilience     | Primary                    | CDRRMC representation, barangay participation, CSO and private-sector partnerships.     |\n| 8\\. Increase Infrastructure Resilience                             | Supporting                 | Cross-office coordination on critical facilities and lifeline continuity.               |\n| 9\\. Ensure Effective Disaster Response                             | Primary                    | EOC mandate, coordination arrangements and institutional decision pathways.             |\n| 10\\. Expedite Recovery and Build Back Better                       | Supporting                 | Governance continuity and accountability for recovery and reconstruction.               |\n\nThe City shall use this alignment to ensure that institutional improvements contribute to the wider urban resilience agenda, not merely to the internal functionality of the CDRRMO and EOC.\n\n# 11. Institutional Improvement Priorities Arising from the Assessment\n\nThe following actions are directly linked to the assessment findings and shall be included in the Institutional Improvement Action Plan.\n\nPriority is the workbook''s gap-priority band: (4 − score) × weight, × 1.5 for mandatory items; High is 9 or more and Medium is 4 or more. Owners, targets and status follow the IAT v1.7 Action Plan.\n\n**Table 1.5**\n\n*Institutional Improvement Priorities and Action Plan Targets*\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 9%" />\n<col style="width: 11%" />\n<col style="width: 35%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Priority</strong></th>\n<th><strong>IAT ref (score)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Required action</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Accountable owner</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Supporting actors</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Target / status</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class="odd">\n<td>High</td>\n<td>A1.1 (2)</td>\n<td>Enact an ordinance establishing the CDRRMO as a regular department with the JMC 2014-1 divisions; attach the Chapter 2 organogram and staffing pattern.</td>\n<td>Mayor / SP (legislative process)</td>\n<td>CDRRMO Head; CLO; HRMO; SP Committee on DRRM</td>\n<td><p>Q1 2027 (SP filing)</p>\n<p>Not started</p></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="even">\n<td>High</td>\n<td>A1.8 (1)</td>\n<td>Establish the State of Imminent Disaster procedure: PDRA trigger, CDRRMC resolution, request to RDRRMC VIII, Mayor''s EO template, adoption rule, lifting and the 45-day report. Test it in a tabletop exercise.</td>\n<td>Mayor (declaration); CDRRMO Head (process)</td>\n<td>CDRRMC; OCD VIII; CLO; Budget Office</td>\n<td><p>Jan 2027 (with MOP)</p>\n<p>Ongoing</p></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<td>High</td>\n<td>B1.1 (2)</td>\n<td>Issue an EO establishing the City EOC under NDRRMC Memo 131 s.2018: the 24/7 OpCen becomes the EOC on activation; the EOC links clusters and the IMT.</td>\n<td>Mayor</td>\n<td>CDRRMO; CLO; CDRRMC</td>\n<td><p>Jan 2027 (with MOP)</p>\n<p>Ongoing</p></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="even">\n<td>High</td>\n<td>B6.1 (1)</td>\n<td>Define the ICS–EOC interface in the ICS EO: the EOC supports the Incident Commander and does not command.</td>\n<td>CDRRMO Head</td>\n<td>Mayor''s Office; CLO; response clusters</td>\n<td><p>Jan 2027 (with MOP)</p>\n<p>Ongoing</p></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<td>High</td>\n<td>A2.1 (2)</td>\n<td>Adopt the JMC 2014-1 organogram (Head + 3 divisions) with separate normal-operations and EOC roles, through the A1.1 ordinance and Chapter 2.</td>\n<td>CDRRMO Head</td>\n<td>HRMO</td>\n<td><p>Jan 2027 (with MOP)</p>\n<p>Ongoing</p></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="even">\n<td>High</td>\n<td>B1.4 / B4.1 / B6.1 (1–2)</td>\n<td>Adopt the scenario-based EOC structure, activation levels and scenario modules (section 6.3) in the EOC EO and each contingency plan; test them in an exercise.</td>\n<td>CDRRMO Head</td>\n<td>CDRRMC; partner offices; OCD VIII</td>\n<td><p>Jan 2027 (with MOP)</p>\n<p>Not started</p></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>A4.4 / A10.4 (2)</td>\n<td>Table the 2024–2026 LDRRMF split by thematic area; adopt the budget-cycle RACI (Chapter 2, 2.16.2); start a quarterly results-and-finance report.</td>\n<td>CDRRMO Head</td>\n<td>All divisions; Budget Office; CDRRMC</td>\n<td><p>Next CDRRMC meeting</p>\n<p>Not started</p></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="even">\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>A1.6 (3)</td>\n<td>Write the State of Calamity protocol and templates; require each QRF resolution to cite the declaration relied on; validate the QRF trigger with the CLO and COA.</td>\n<td>CDRRMO Research and Planning</td>\n<td>CDRRMC; CLO; RDANA team</td>\n<td><p>Jan 2027 (with MOP)</p>\n<p>Ongoing</p></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>A1.2 (3)</td>\n<td>Issue an amending EO to EO 2024-032A: add the City College of Ormoc, restore an academe seat, fill the third private-sector seat and cite SP accreditation of each CSO. Review the roster yearly.</td>\n<td>Mayor</td>\n<td>CDRRMO (Secretariat); CLO</td>\n<td><p>Dec 2026</p>\n<p>Ongoing</p></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="even">\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>A1.3 / A1.4 (3)</td>\n<td>Adopt the Secretariat SOP and resolution register; have the Mayor designate the presiding officer in writing; correct and certify the 2025 resolutions; clean the 2021–2025 resolution and EO files.</td>\n<td>CDRRMO Head (Secretariat)</td>\n<td>CDRRMC members</td>\n<td><p>Dec 2026; quarterly</p>\n<p>Ongoing</p></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>A1.5 (3)</td>\n<td>Amend the evacuation ordinance (triggers linked to the warning–action matrix, authority to order, assistance to PWD and older persons, livestock and pets, enforcement); add barangay-level class and work suspension; add hazard overlay zones in the CLUP/ZO update.</td>\n<td>Mayor / SP</td>\n<td>CDRRMO; CLO; CPDO; barangays</td>\n<td><p>Q2 2027</p>\n<p>Not started</p></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="even">\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>A1.7 / A4.6 (3)</td>\n<td>Use the CDC and LFC seats to secure the anticipatory-action line and the EOC budget in the FY2028 budget call; file minutes that record CDRRMO input.</td>\n<td>CDRRMO Head</td>\n<td>Budget Office; Accounting; BAC</td>\n<td><p>2027 budget call</p>\n<p>Ongoing</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n*Note.* Owners, targets and status follow the IAT v1.7 Action Plan; confirm them at the December 2026 validation workshop.\n\nThe action plan should retain the detailed status, funding, target dates and evidence requirements in the workbook. This chapter establishes the governance requirements and ownership; it does not replace the Action Plan.\n\n## 11.1 Priority Sequencing\n\nThe improvement actions shall be implemented in the following sequence:\n\nPhase 1 – Legal and institutional foundation\n\n- CDRRMO enabling ordinance;\n\n- Updated CDRRMC Executive Order;\n\n- EOC establishment issuance;\n\n- State of Imminent Disaster SOP;\n\n- State of Calamity SOP.\n\nPhase 2 – Operational institutionalization\n\n- Secretariat SOP and action register;\n\n<!-- -->\n\n- Clean-up of the CDRRMC resolution and Executive Order files for 2021–2025;\n\n<!-- -->\n\n- Formal inter-agency coordination and delegation arrangements;\n\n- Approved CDRRMO and EOC role descriptions;\n\n- Institutional policy and records register.\n\nPhase 3 – Performance and continuous improvement\n\n- CDRRMC governance performance monitoring;\n\n- CAPA implementation;\n\n- Periodic legal and policy review;\n\n- Validation and testing of institutional procedures.\n\n# 12. Required SOPs, Forms and Institutional Records\n\nThe following instruments shall be maintained under this chapter.\n\n| **Code** | **SOP / Instrument**                               | **Required output**                                      |\n|----------|----------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------|\n| GOV-01   | CDRRMC governance and meeting procedure            | Annual calendar, notices, agendas and minutes            |\n| GOV-02   | CDRRMC Secretariat management                      | Secretariat register and document-control system         |\n| GOV-03   | Institutional policy and ordinance development     | Policy development and legal review checklist            |\n| GOV-04   | State of Calamity declaration and lifting          | RDANA-based recommendation and declaration package       |\n| GOV-05   | State of Imminent Disaster declaration and lifting | PDRA, regional request, Mayor''s EO and reporting package |\n| GOV-06   | Inter-agency and vertical coordination             | Coordination protocol and contact directory              |\n| GOV-07   | RACI and delegation management                     | Responsibility and authority register                    |\n| GOV-08   | Institutional records management                   | Controlled document and records register                 |\n| GOV-09   | Governance performance monitoring                  | KPI dashboard and action register                        |\n| GOV-10   | Corrective and preventive action management        | CAPA register and verification reports                   |\n\nThe following templates shall be included in the MOO annexes:\n\n- GOV-F01 – CDRRMC Membership and Designation Register;\n\n- GOV-F02 – CDRRMC Meeting Notice and Agenda;\n\n- GOV-F03 – CDRRMC Attendance and Minutes;\n\n- GOV-F04 – CDRRMC Resolution Template;\n\n- GOV-F05 – Resolution and Action Monitoring Register;\n\n- GOV-F06 – Institutional Policy Review Checklist;\n\n- GOV-F07 – State of Calamity Technical Assessment;\n\n- GOV-F08 – State of Calamity Recommendation and Resolution;\n\n- GOV-F09 – State of Imminent Disaster PDRA Summary;\n\n- GOV-F10 – Request to RDRRMC VIII;\n\n- GOV-F11 – Mayor''s State of Imminent Disaster Executive Order Template;\n\n- GOV-F12 – State of Imminent Disaster Lifting Documentation;\n\n- GOV-F13 – Post-Lifting 45-Day Report;\n\n- GOV-F14 – Institutional RACI Register;\n\n- GOV-F15 – Corrective and Preventive Action Register;\n\n- GOV-F16 – Legal and Policy Register.\n\nAll templates shall have document identifiers, version numbers, approval status, responsible custodian and review dates.\n\n# 13. Governance Controls, Documentation and Records Management\n\nThe CDRRMO shall establish a controlled documentation system to ensure that institutional decisions and procedures remain accessible, authentic, traceable and current.\n\n![Figure 17: 13. Governance Controls, Documentation and Records Management](/library/ch1v12/fig-17.webp)\n\n## 13.1 Document Control\n\nEach controlled document shall contain:\n\n- Document title and code;\n\n- Version and revision number;\n\n- Date of preparation;\n\n- Prepared-by and reviewed-by designations;\n\n- Approving authority;\n\n- Effective date;\n\n- Revision history; and\n\n- Distribution and access classification.\n\nOnly approved versions shall be used as official operational references.\n\n## 13.2 Records Management\n\nThe CDRRMO Secretariat shall maintain the official governance records in coordination with the City Records Officer.\n\nRecords shall be classified into:\n\n1.  Legal and policy records;\n\n2.  CDRRMC governance records;\n\n3.  Institutional organizational records;\n\n4.  Declaration and executive decision records;\n\n5.  Financial and resource accountability records;\n\n6.  Coordination and partnership records;\n\n7.  Performance and audit records; and\n\n8.  Corrective action and improvement records.\n\nRetention and disposition shall follow applicable government records management and archival requirements.\n\n## 13.3 Data Protection and Information Sharing\n\nThe CDRRMO shall coordinate with the City''s Data Protection Officer and information technology office to establish appropriate safeguards for DRRM information.\n\nThe system shall distinguish:\n\n- Publicly available risk and preparedness information;\n\n- Internal institutional information;\n\n- Restricted operational information;\n\n- Personal information of affected populations;\n\n- Sensitive personal information; and\n\n- Confidential personnel and security records.\n\nThe EOC shall use aggregated and appropriately anonymized information for operational dashboards whenever personal identifiers are not required.\n\nData sharing with CSWDO, CPDO, national agencies, barangays and partners shall be governed by appropriate data-sharing agreements and privacy safeguards under RA 10173.\n\n# 14. Implementation, Review and Institutional Assurance\n\n## 14.1 Institutional Ownership\n\nThe CDRRMO Head shall be the overall process owner for the implementation of this chapter, under the administrative authority of the Local Chief Executive and the policy oversight of the CDRRMC.\n\nThe City Legal Office, HRMO, Budget Office, Accounting Office, Secretariat and other concerned institutions shall discharge their assigned responsibilities.\n\n## 14.2 Review Schedule\n\n| **Review activity**              | **Frequency**                                      | **Responsible owner**      |\n|----------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------|----------------------------|\n| CDRRMC membership review         | Annually and after material organizational changes | CDRRMO                     |\n| Governance SOP review            | Annually and after significant policy changes      | CDRRMO Head                |\n| Legal and policy register review | At least annually                                  | CDRRMO / City Legal Office |\n| RACI and delegation review       | Annually and after structural changes              | CDRRMO / HRMO              |\n| CDRRMC performance review        | Quarterly, with annual consolidation               | CDRRMC Secretariat         |\n| Institutional assessment         | Annually, or after major institutional changes     | CDRRMO                     |\n| CAPA review                      | Quarterly                                          | CDRRMO Head                |\n| MOO revision                     | Annually or when necessary                         | CDRRMO Head                |\n\n## 14.3 Institutional Assurance\n\nThe implementation of this chapter shall be evaluated through three complementary tests:\n\nCompliance test: Are statutory and regulatory requirements met?\n\nEffectiveness test: Do institutional arrangements produce timely, coordinated and reliable decisions and outputs?\n\nSustainability test: Are the arrangements institutionalized, documented, resourced, tested and capable of surviving leadership and personnel changes?\n\nA governance process shall be considered effective only when it satisfies all three dimensions appropriate to its function.\n\n# 15. Technical Validation Notes and Outstanding Decisions\n\nThe following matters should be resolved before Chapter 1 is submitted for formal approval.\n\n| **Validation issue**       | **Required action**                                                                                                                                                    | **Responsible institution**  |\n|----------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------|\n| CDRRMO legal status        | Confirm existing EO and prepare enabling ordinance.                                                                                                                    | City Legal Office / SP       |\n| CDRRMC composition         | Verify all statutory seats and CSO accreditation.                                                                                                                      | CDRRMO / Mayor''s Office      |\n| State of Calamity          | Validate local declaration and lifting templates against RA 10121 Section 16.                                                                                          | City Legal Office            |\n| State of Imminent Disaster | Confirm certified IRR provisions, RDRRMC VIII request route, response timelines and local EO procedure.                                                                | OCD VIII / City Legal Office |\n| ICC coordination           | Confirm applicability of provincial coordination provisions to Ormoc.                                                                                                  | OCD VIII                     |\n| EOC establishment          | Verify NDRRMC Memorandum No. 131, s. 2018 and the exact issuance title and provisions.                                                                                         | OCD VIII                     |\n| EOC–ICS interface          | Confirm the relationship between EOC, IMT, Incident Commander and response clusters.                                                                                   | CDRRMO / OCD VIII            |\n| Organizational structure   | Reconcile JMC 2014-1, approved plantilla and proposed CDRRMO department structure.                                                                                     | HRMO / CDRRMO                |\n| Institutional maturity     | Validate provisional scores and resolve contradictory evidence.                                                                                                        | Assessment team / CDRRMO     |\n| Version control            | The workbook is now v1-7 (updated 11 November 2026), while its Guide sheet still identifies the instrument as Version 1.1. Reconcile the workbook version and revision history. | Assessment team              |\n\nTwo important corrections incorporated into this revised chapter:\n\n1.  A State of Calamity is declared by the local sanggunian upon CDRRMC recommendation, not simply through a Mayor''s Executive Order.\n\n2.  A State of Imminent Disaster is a separate anticipatory mechanism under RA 12287. The Local Chief Executive may declare it through an Executive Order upon the prescribed Regional DRRM Council recommendation.\n\nThese distinctions are essential to legal compliance.\n\n# 16. Relationship with the Succeeding Chapters\n\nTo avoid duplication and maintain a coherent Manual, the following division of responsibilities is recommended:\n\n| **Chapter** | **Subject**                                                             | **Principal assessment references** |\n|-------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------|\n| 1           | Legal Mandate, DRRM Governance and Institutional Accountability         | A1, B1.1, C1–C5 interfaces          |\n| 2           | CDRRMO Organizational Structure, Staffing and Human Resource Management | A2, B4                              |\n| 3           | Risk Intelligence, Planning and Institutional Preparedness              | A3, A5, relevant Part C indicators  |\n| 4           | Early Warning, Activation, Incident Management and Emergency Response   | A6, A7, A8, B1.2–B1.5               |\n| 5           | DRRM Financing, Procurement and Resource Accountability                 | A4                                  |\n| 6           | EOC Operations, Information Management, Logistics and Coordination      | B2–B7, relevant A indicators        |\n| 7           | Monitoring, Evaluation, Knowledge Management and Continuous Improvement | A5.4–A5.9, A10                      |\n| 8           | Recovery, Rehabilitation and Build Back Better                          | A9                                  |\n| 9           | Partnerships, Local Ownership and Institutional Sustainability          | A10, relevant Part C indicators     |\n\nThis structure should be reconciled with the ten functional areas in Annex A and the eight contractual functional modules. The chapter numbering is a proposed detailed document architecture, not a change to the contractual scope.\n\n# 16. Concluding Notes\n\nThis chapter establishes the legal, institutional and governance foundation for the integrated operation of Ormoc City''s CDRRMC, CDRRMO and EOC.\n\nIt recognizes that urban resilience is not produced by a single office or emergency facility. It emerges from the effective interaction of executive leadership, institutional capacity, policy coordination, risk information, financial governance, community participation and operational systems.\n\nThe institutional assessment demonstrates that Ormoc has established important DRRM governance practices, particularly regular CDRRMC meetings, Secretariat functions, DRRM representation in development and financial bodies, and existing planning and partnership mechanisms. However, gaps in legal institutionalization, EOC authority, anticipatory governance and cross-office coordination continue to constrain the effectiveness of the wider system.\n\nThe provisions of this chapter respond to these issues by establishing:\n\n- Clear institutional mandates and decision rights;\n\n- Defined process ownership and responsibility assignments;\n\n- Standardized governance workflows;\n\n- Legally differentiated declaration procedures;\n\n- Formalized institutional and inter-agency interfaces;\n\n- RACI-based accountability;\n\n- TQM and PDCA-based quality assurance; and\n\n- Evidence-based monitoring and continuous improvement.\n\nThe ultimate objective is to move Ormoc City''s DRRM governance from arrangements that are partly dependent on individual initiative toward a legally grounded, process-driven, integrated and continuously improving institutional system.\n\nThis foundation shall support the implementation of the succeeding modules, particularly organizational management, preparedness, EOC operations, information management, resource mobilization, recovery and performance improvement.\n\n# References\n\nThe following references provide the legal and technical foundation for this chapter. The final bibliography should be consolidated with the complete MOO reference register.\n\n- City Government of Ormoc, City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office. (2026). *CDRRMO reference: Local policies* [Shared Drive folder of the City''s DRRM policy instruments: Sangguniang Panlungsod ordinances, executive orders, CDRRMC resolutions and SP resolutions]. https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/12NWwUTAE6ub1vW31M2NSMktYlP-BP4oR\n\n- City Government of Ormoc, Sangguniang Panlungsod. (1993–2025). *SP ordinances* [Shared Drive folder, by year, including Ordinance No. 008, s. 2014 establishing the CDRRMO and Ordinance No. 011, s. 2015 amending it]. https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/132g-VeSpXkorcNCte2fpyVY3Az3baBXS\n\n- City Government of Ormoc. (2026). Ormoc CDRRMO–EOC Institutional Assessment Tool (v1-7, 11 November 2026). Unpublished assessment workbook.\n\n- City Government of Ormoc, City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office. (2026). CDRRMO organizational chart and internal staff papers on planning, budgeting and cluster-based barangay coordination. Unpublished working papers.\n\n- National Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council. (2018). Memorandum No. 131, s. 2018: Guidelines on the establishment, operationalization and management of emergency operations centers.\n\n- City Government of Ormoc, City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council. (2021–2025). CDRRMC resolutions, including Res. Nos. 01–12, 2-A, 2-B, 14 and 15 s.2021; 01–09 s.2022; 01–07 s.2023; 01–06, 09 and 10 s.2024; 01–08 s.2025.\n\n- City Government of Ormoc, Office of the City Mayor. (2022–2024). Executive Order Nos. 2022-0006, 2022-00020, 2023-0041 and 2024-032A.\n\n- Sangguniang Panlungsod ng Ormoc. (2021). Resolution Nos. 2021-036, 2021-149, 2021-357 and 2021-365 to 2021-369.\n\n<!-- -->\n\n- Department of Budget and Management, Department of the Interior and Local Government, Civil Service Commission, & National Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council. (2014). Joint Memorandum Circular No. 2014-1: Implementing guidelines for the establishment of Local Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Offices or Barangay Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Committees in Local Government Units. [<u>Official DBM issuance</u>](https://www.dbm.gov.ph/wp-content/uploads/Issuances/2014/Joint%20Memorandum%20Circular/JMC2014-1%20_LDRRMOs.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com).\n\n- National Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council. (2012). Memorandum Circular No. 4, s. 2012: Implementing guidelines on the use of the Incident Command System.\n\n- National Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council. (2026). Implementing Rules and Regulations of Republic Act No. 12287. Signed September 14, 2026, as identified in the assessment workbook.\n\n- Republic of the Philippines. (1991). Republic Act No. 7160: Local Government Code of 1991.\n\n- Republic of the Philippines. (2009). Republic Act No. 9729: Climate Change Act of 2009.\n\n- Republic of the Philippines. (2010). Republic Act No. 10121: Philippine Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Act of 2010. [<u>Full text of RA 10121</u>](https://lawphil.net/statutes/repacts/ra2010/ra_10121_2010.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com).\n\n- Republic of the Philippines. (2012). Republic Act No. 10173: Data Privacy Act of 2012.\n\n- Republic of the Philippines. (2018). Republic Act No. 11036: Mental Health Act.\n\n- Republic of the Philippines. (2024). Republic Act No. 12076: Ligtas Pinoy Centers Act.\n\n- Republic of the Philippines. (2024). Republic Act No. 12009: New Government Procurement Act.\n\n- Republic of the Philippines. (2025). Republic Act No. 12287: Declaration of State of Imminent Disaster Act. [<u>Official legislative reference</u>](https://ldr.senate.gov.ph/legislative-issuance/republic-act-no-12287?utm_source=chatgpt.com).\n\n- United Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction. (2015). Sendai Framework for Disaster Risk Reduction 2015–2030.\n\n- United Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction. (2017). Disaster Resilience Scorecard for Cities: Detailed level assessment.\n\n- United Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction. (2018). Making cities resilient 2030: Ten Essentials for Making Cities Resilient.\n\n- International Organization for Standardization. (2015). ISO 9001:2015: Quality management systems—Requirements.\n\n- International Organization for Standardization. (2018). ISO 31000:2018: Risk management—Guidelines.\n\n- International Organization for Standardization. (2019). ISO 22301:2019: Security and resilience—Business continuity management systems—Requirements.\n\n# INSTITUTIONAL IMPLEMENTATION PACKAGE\n\nThis package gives operational effect to Chapter 1 through a coordinated set of proposed legislative and executive instruments, standard operating procedures, responsibility matrices and governance templates. It is an integrated annex set and must be read with Chapter 1, particularly Sections 6–8, 11–14.\n\n# DOCUMENT CONTROL AND APPROVAL\n\n| **Control field**         | **Designation / entry**                                | **Control field**             | **Designation / entry**           |\n|---------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------|-----------------------------------|\n| **Document owner**        | CDRRMO Head                                            | **Document custodian**        | CDRRMO Secretariat                |\n| **Technical review**      | CDRRMO / EOC / concerned offices                       | **Legal review**              | City Legal Office                 |\n| **Administrative review** | HRMO / Budget / Accounting / BAC                       | **External technical review** | OCD Region VIII, as applicable    |\n| **Approval**              | Mayor / CDRRMC / Sanggunian, as legally applicable     | **Effective date**            | Upon valid approval / issuance    |\n| **Review cycle**          | Annual and upon material legal or institutional change | **Supersedes**                | None – initial consolidated draft |\n\n# Revision History\n\n| **Version** | **Date**    | **Description**                                                                                     | **Prepared / reviewed by**            |\n|-------------|-------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------|\n| 0.9         | 31 Oct 2026 | Integrated working draft based on uploaded Chapter 1 and assessment-informed implementation package | Prepared for institutional validation |\n\n**Approval status**: This document is a controlled working draft. No provision becomes an official City policy, ordinance, executive order or operational directive until approved and issued by the authority legally competent to do so.\n\n# Annex Register and Cross-Reference Guide\n\n| **Annex** | **Instrument**                                                                                                                                                                        | **Approval / control route**           |\n|-----------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------|\n| 1-A       | [**<u>Proposed CDRRMO Institutionalization Ordinance</u>**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/13cMSMcBS_Qt9uTdUosDUldCAKx7FHC1D4Bzt5FHGT9c/edit?usp=sharing)                         | Sangguniang Panlungsod                 |\n| 1-B       | [**<u>Proposed CDRRMC Reconstitution Executive Order</u>**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Nkl8beo4GWOFOGEgj65SvMvUASluGTdu7gf1yWO-grI/edit?usp=sharing)                         | City Mayor                             |\n| 1-C       | [**<u>Proposed EOC Establishment Executive Order</u>**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/18uBR_tIk_zjFzo9psgwuNZKmu_mPO0F5hL5e7mXXUnA/edit?usp=sharing)                             | City Mayor                             |\n| 1-D       | [**<u>SOP GOV-04: State of Calamity Declaration and Lifting</u>**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1W3hAQEyytFf9Z_7q4Ao3bjYEJUVK-zCHxrXx_unQmnQ/edit?usp=sharing)                  | Approval under City delegation         |\n| 1-E       | [**<u>SOP GOV-05: State of Imminent Disaster / Anticipatory Action</u>**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1KbynqOdQcDhj3HEMcG8Qd4pzK3BNU6FOkNXCBNHKZ4E/edit?usp=sharing)           | Approval after RA 12287 IRR validation |\n| 1-F       | [**<u>Master RACI and Institutional Interface Matrix</u>**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1OYJ_I8C48HPABRPQUthSVnfmgAyy1fKbfrs9U_TuA0g/edit?usp=sharing)                         | CDRRMC / City approval                 |\n| 1-G       | [**<u>Governance Forms GOV-F01 to GOV-F16</u>**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hiaMKRrolVp4U3_Q_mLzhoV4lfQVQaqz/edit?usp=sharing&ouid=108981648814069460632&rtpof=true&sd=true) | CDRRMO Secretariat custodian           |\n| 1-H       | [**<u>Implementation Roadmap</u>**](https://docs.google.com/document/d/10SkTHJ-g0ttLrsfXFrPfwVzjkJX2HZLBRBlBmTL1aUc/edit?usp=sharing)                                                 | City Mayor                             |\n\n**Consistent definitions**. CDRRMC means City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council; CDRRMO means permanent City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office;\n'
